# Level 6 → 7 — Server-Wide Search with Ownership Filters

## Level Goal
Find the password file stored somewhere on the server, owned by user `bandit7`, group `bandit6`, and exactly 33 bytes in size.

## Description
This level extends `find` to a filesystem-wide search and introduces stderr redirection to filter noisy permission-denied errors from the output.

## Approach
I ran a full-server search from the root directory:

```bash
find / -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null
```

Without `2>/dev/null`, the output was flooded with "Permission denied" errors. Redirecting stderr cleaned the results, revealing a single match:

```
/var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password
```

I read the file directly:

```bash
cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password
```

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## Key Takeaway
`2>/dev/null` suppresses noise while keeping real results visible. The `-user` and `-group` flags are directly applicable to privilege escalation enumeration on real systems.
